(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("finops",{"finops/the-finops-cycle-inform-optimize-operate":{"html":"\u003cp>Cloud makes spending a daily engineering decision. Every instance type, replica count and retention setting is a small purchase. FinOps is the habit of making those purchases visible, deliberate and shared between engineering, finance and product.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The three phases: inform, optimize and operate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The people involved and what each owns\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Unit economics: cost per request, per customer, per build\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Maturity: crawl, walk and run\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why cost is a reliability and design topic too\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-three-phases\">The three phases\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Inform means everyone can see what is being spent and by whom. Optimize means acting on that data by removing waste, choosing better prices and improving the design. Operate means turning the work into routines, budgets and reviews. The cycle repeats, because new services and new price lists keep arriving.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-does-what\">Who does what\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Engineers decide how much is used, because they choose sizes, counts and architecture. Finance forecasts and reports. A central FinOps or platform group supplies data, tooling and training. Product owners decide what is worth paying for. Nobody can fix cost alone, so the first job is agreeing who can act on each line of the bill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"unit-economics\">Unit economics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A bill that grows by twenty percent is good news if traffic grew by fifty percent and bad news if traffic was flat. Divide cost by something the business understands, such as cost per thousand requests, per active customer or per pipeline run. Track the unit cost over time, and a team can see whether it is becoming more efficient.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"maturity\">Maturity\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>At crawl, teams have tags on some resources and a monthly report. At walk, allocation is mostly complete, budgets exist and teams receive their own numbers weekly. At run, cost is part of design reviews, anomalies are alerted on within a day and unit cost targets are set per service. Do not try to jump to run in one quarter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cost-as-a-design-property\">Cost as a design property\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cost, latency and reliability trade against each other. A multi region database costs more and fails less often. A larger cache costs more and saves compute. Write the cost next to the other design numbers in the review, so the trade is made on purpose and not discovered on an invoice.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"use&gt;data;data&gt;act;act&gt;unit\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The FinOps cycle: inform, optimize, operate\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"use\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Engineering decisions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Billing data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Optimize and operate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"unit\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Unit cost\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Engineering decisions to Billing data; 2, Billing data to Optimize and operate; 3, Optimize and operate to Unit cost.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"use\">\u003cdt>Engineering decisions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sizes, counts, retention.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Made blind.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>Billing data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cost by owner.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unallocated.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Optimize and operate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Savings and budgets.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No follow up.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"unit\">\u003cdt>Unit cost \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cost per unit of value.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-bill-that-nobody-could-explain\">Incident: A bill that nobody could explain\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>The monthly cloud bill rose by thirty percent and finance asked engineering for the reason.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-11-01 --granularity MONTHLY --metrics UnblendedCost --group-by Type=DIMENSION,Key=SERVICE\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-11-01 --granularity MONTHLY --metrics UnblendedCost --group-by Type=DIMENSION,Key=SERVICE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">EC2 - Other        41000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Amazon S3          18500\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Data Transfer       9200\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The increase was real, but no one owned the largest lines and nothing linked cost to traffic, so the discussion lasted two weeks. Once the team reported cost per thousand requests next to the bill, they saw that traffic had grown faster than cost, and the real problem was one test environment that had been left running. They then agreed owners for each service line and a weekly review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Looking only at the total bill\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating FinOps as a finance project only\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Cutting cost without checking reliability\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Starting with tools before agreeing owners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reporting once a quarter instead of weekly\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-three-phases","text":"The three phases"},{"id":"who-does-what","text":"Who does what"},{"id":"unit-economics","text":"Unit economics"},{"id":"maturity","text":"Maturity"},{"id":"cost-as-a-design-property","text":"Cost as a design property"},{"id":"incident-a-bill-that-nobody-could-explain","text":"Incident: A bill that nobody could explain"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"FinOps Foundation framework","u":"https://www.finops.org/framework/"},{"t":"OpenCost","u":"https://www.opencost.io/docs/"},{"t":"Azure Cost Management","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/"}],"text":"Cloud makes spending a daily engineering decision. Every instance type, replica count and retention setting is a small purchase. FinOps is the habit of making those purchases visible, deliberate and shared between engineering, finance and product. What you will learn The three phases: inform, optimize and operate The people involved and what each owns Unit economics: cost per request, per customer, per build Maturity: crawl, walk and run Why cost is a reliability and design topic too The three phases Inform means everyone can see what is being spent and by whom. Optimize means acting on that data by removing waste, choosing better prices and improving the design. Operate means turning the work into routines, budgets and reviews. The cycle repeats, because new services and new price lists keep arriving. Who does what Engineers decide how much is used, because they choose sizes, counts and architecture. Finance forecasts and reports. A central FinOps or platform group supplies data, tooling and training. Product owners decide what is worth paying for. Nobody can fix cost alone, so the first job is agreeing who can act on each line of the bill. Unit economics A bill that grows by twenty percent is good news if traffic grew by fifty percent and bad news if traffic was flat. Divide cost by something the business understands, such as cost per thousand requests, per active customer or per pipeline run. Track the unit cost over time, and a team can see whether it is becoming more efficient. Maturity At crawl, teams have tags on some resources and a monthly report. At walk, allocation is mostly complete, budgets exist and teams receive their own numbers weekly. At run, cost is part of design reviews, anomalies are alerted on within a day and unit cost targets are set per service. Do not try to jump to run in one quarter. Cost as a design property Cost, latency and reliability trade against each other. A multi region database costs more and fails less often. A larger cache costs more and saves compute. Write the cost next to the other design numbers in the review, so the trade is made on purpose and not discovered on an invoice. The FinOps cycle: inform, optimize, operate Flow in order: 1, Engineering decisions to Billing data; 2, Billing data to Optimize and operate; 3, Optimize and operate to Unit cost. Step Data store Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Engineering decisions Step What it does: Sizes, counts, retention. If it fails or is weak: Made blind. Billing data Data store What it does: Cost by owner. If it fails or is weak: Unallocated. Optimize and operate Step What it does: Savings and budgets. If it fails or is weak: No follow up. Unit cost Observability What it does: Cost per unit of value. If it fails or is weak: Ignored. Incident: A bill that nobody could explain Incident The monthly cloud bill rose by thirty percent and finance asked engineering for the reason. $ aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-11-01 --granularity MONTHLY --metrics UnblendedCost --group-by Type=DIMENSION,Key=SERVICE EC2 - Other 41000 Amazon S3 18500 Data Transfer 9200 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The increase was real, but no one owned the largest lines and nothing linked cost to traffic, so the discussion lasted two weeks. Once the team reported cost per thousand requests next to the bill, they saw that traffic had grown faster than cost, and the real problem was one test environment that had been left running. They then agreed owners for each service line and a weekly review. Common mistakes Looking only at the total bill Treating FinOps as a finance project only Cutting cost without checking reliability Starting with tools before agreeing owners Reporting once a quarter instead of weekly"},"finops/billing-data-tags-allocation-and-the-focus-format":{"html":"\u003cp>Good decisions need good data. The cloud invoice is a summary, and the detailed export is where cost becomes explainable. This lesson covers what is in that export and how to make every line belong to someone.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What the detailed billing exports contain\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tag and label design that survives reality\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allocating by account, project and namespace\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Splitting shared costs such as networking and support\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The FOCUS format for comparing clouds\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-exports\">The exports\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>AWS provides the Cost and Usage Report, Azure provides cost exports from Cost Management and Google Cloud provides a billing export to BigQuery. Each row is a usage record with a time, a service, a usage amount, a price and the identifiers of the resource. Load them into a warehouse so that you can query them with SQL and keep history longer than the console does.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"tags-and-labels\">Tags and labels\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pick a small required set: owner team, service name, environment and cost centre. Enforce them at creation time with policy or infrastructure code, because tags added later by hand are always incomplete. Keep values from a controlled list, since team names spelled three ways make three owners. Note that some costs cannot be tagged, such as parts of data transfer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"allocation-structure\">Allocation structure\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The most reliable allocation is structural. A separate account, subscription or project per team and environment makes the bill owner obvious without any tags. Inside Kubernetes, allocate by namespace and label, using the requests of each workload. Use tags to refine and structure to guarantee.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shared-costs\">Shared costs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some costs belong to everyone: networking hubs, logging, security tools, support plans and the platform team itself. Choose a rule and publish it, such as splitting in proportion to each team direct spend or to usage of the shared service. A simple, stable rule that people understand beats a perfect one that nobody trusts.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-focus-format\">The FOCUS format\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>FOCUS is an open specification that defines common column names and meanings for billing data from different providers, such as BilledCost, EffectiveCost and ResourceId. Using it makes multi cloud reports and tooling easier, because a query does not have to be rewritten for each provider.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"exp&gt;wh;wh&gt;alloc;alloc&gt;rep\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Billing data, tags, allocation and the FOCUS format\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"exp\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Billing export\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"wh\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Warehouse\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"alloc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Allocation rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rep\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Team reports\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Billing export to Warehouse; 2, Warehouse to Allocation rules; 3, Allocation rules to Team reports.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"exp\">\u003cdt>Billing export \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Line items.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing fields.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"wh\">\u003cdt>Warehouse \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> SQL and history.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Late data.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"alloc\">\u003cdt>Allocation rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tags and accounts.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Untagged.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rep\">\u003cdt>Team reports \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Cost by owner.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Distrusted.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-forty-percent-of-cost-with-no-owner\">Incident: Forty percent of cost with no owner\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A finance review found that almost half of the monthly cost had no owner tag.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-11-01 --granularity MONTHLY --metrics UnblendedCost --group-by Type=TAG,Key=owner\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-11-01 --granularity MONTHLY --metrics UnblendedCost --group-by Type=TAG,Key=owner\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">owner$              52000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">owner$platform      21000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">owner$search        14000\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The first row is cost with an empty owner tag, and it was the largest line. Resources created by older scripts and by hand had no tags, and the tag policy only covered new resources of some types. The team added a policy that blocks creation without the required tags, a weekly report of untagged spend by account and a clean up sprint, and the unowned share fell to seven percent in two months.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Optional tags that nobody enforces\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Free text tag values with many spellings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allocating shared cost with a rule nobody understands\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Only using the console and not the export\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming every cost can be tagged\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-exports","text":"The exports"},{"id":"tags-and-labels","text":"Tags and labels"},{"id":"allocation-structure","text":"Allocation structure"},{"id":"shared-costs","text":"Shared costs"},{"id":"the-focus-format","text":"The FOCUS format"},{"id":"incident-forty-percent-of-cost-with-no-owner","text":"Incident: Forty percent of cost with no owner"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"FinOps Foundation: FOCUS","u":"https://www.finops.org/focus/"},{"t":"AWS Cost and Usage Reports","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/cur/latest/userguide/what-is-cur.html"},{"t":"AWS cost allocation tags","u":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/awsaccountbilling/latest/aboutv2/cost-alloc-tags.html"}],"text":"Good decisions need good data. The cloud invoice is a summary, and the detailed export is where cost becomes explainable. This lesson covers what is in that export and how to make every line belong to someone. What you will learn What the detailed billing exports contain Tag and label design that survives reality Allocating by account, project and namespace Splitting shared costs such as networking and support The FOCUS format for comparing clouds The exports AWS provides the Cost and Usage Report, Azure provides cost exports from Cost Management and Google Cloud provides a billing export to BigQuery. Each row is a usage record with a time, a service, a usage amount, a price and the identifiers of the resource. Load them into a warehouse so that you can query them with SQL and keep history longer than the console does. Tags and labels Pick a small required set: owner team, service name, environment and cost centre. Enforce them at creation time with policy or infrastructure code, because tags added later by hand are always incomplete. Keep values from a controlled list, since team names spelled three ways make three owners. Note that some costs cannot be tagged, such as parts of data transfer. Allocation structure The most reliable allocation is structural. A separate account, subscription or project per team and environment makes the bill owner obvious without any tags. Inside Kubernetes, allocate by namespace and label, using the requests of each workload. Use tags to refine and structure to guarantee. Shared costs Some costs belong to everyone: networking hubs, logging, security tools, support plans and the platform team itself. Choose a rule and publish it, such as splitting in proportion to each team direct spend or to usage of the shared service. A simple, stable rule that people understand beats a perfect one that nobody trusts. The FOCUS format FOCUS is an open specification that defines common column names and meanings for billing data from different providers, such as BilledCost, EffectiveCost and ResourceId. Using it makes multi cloud reports and tooling easier, because a query does not have to be rewritten for each provider. Billing data, tags, allocation and the FOCUS format Flow in order: 1, Billing export to Warehouse; 2, Warehouse to Allocation rules; 3, Allocation rules to Team reports. Data store Step Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Billing export Data store What it does: Line items. If it fails or is weak: Missing fields. Warehouse Data store What it does: SQL and history. If it fails or is weak: Late data. Allocation rules Step What it does: Tags and accounts. If it fails or is weak: Untagged. Team reports Observability What it does: Cost by owner. If it fails or is weak: Distrusted. Incident: Forty percent of cost with no owner Incident A finance review found that almost half of the monthly cost had no owner tag. $ aws ce get-cost-and-usage --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-11-01 --granularity MONTHLY --metrics UnblendedCost --group-by Type=TAG,Key=owner owner$ 52000 owner$platform 21000 owner$search 14000 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The first row is cost with an empty owner tag, and it was the largest line. Resources created by older scripts and by hand had no tags, and the tag policy only covered new resources of some types. The team added a policy that blocks creation without the required tags, a weekly report of untagged spend by account and a clean up sprint, and the unowned share fell to seven percent in two months. Common mistakes Optional tags that nobody enforces Free text tag values with many spellings Allocating shared cost with a rule nobody understands Only using the console and not the export Assuming every cost can be tagged"},"finops/finding-waste-idle-orphaned-and-oversized-resources":{"html":"\u003cp>Waste is the cheapest saving because it needs no change of design. It is also easy to cause an outage while removing it, so the process matters as much as the search.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The usual sources of waste\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rightsizing with real utilisation data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Scheduling non production environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Orphaned volumes, snapshots, addresses and load balancers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A safe removal process with an undo path\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-sources\">Common sources\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Typical waste includes stopped instances that still have paid disks, detached volumes, old snapshots, unused public addresses, idle load balancers, test clusters that nobody uses and over provisioned databases. Each is small, but together they often reach ten to twenty percent of a bill.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rightsizing\">Rightsizing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compare what a resource has with what it uses over a representative period, including peaks such as month end. Look at CPU, memory, disk and network, using percentiles and not only averages. Provider tools give recommendations, and you should confirm them against application behaviour, because memory is often invisible to default metrics. Change one step at a time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"scheduling\">Scheduling\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Development and test environments are needed during working hours and not at night or at weekends. Stopping them automatically on a schedule saves most of their cost. Make the schedule easy to override, so a team working late can start their environment without a ticket.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"orphans\">Orphans\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Resources lose their owner when projects end. Find them with queries such as volumes with no attachment for thirty days or load balancers with no healthy targets. Check the creation time and tags, and contact the owner or the team that owned the account before deleting anything.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"safe-removal\">Safe removal\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a staged process: tag as candidate for deletion with a date, notify the owner, stop or detach first, snapshot where it matters, wait a defined period and then delete. Record each action so that a mistake can be reversed. Never delete on the basis of cost alone without an owner decision.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"scan&gt;cand;cand&gt;stop;stop&gt;del\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Finding waste: idle, orphaned and oversized resources\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"scan\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Utilisation scan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cand\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Candidates\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"stop\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Stop or detach\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"del\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Delete\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Utilisation scan to Candidates; 2, Candidates to Stop or detach; 3, Stop or detach to Delete.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"scan\">\u003cdt>Utilisation scan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Percentiles.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Short window.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cand\">\u003cdt>Candidates \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tag and notify.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"stop\">\u003cdt>Stop or detach \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reversible step.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"del\">\u003cdt>Delete \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> After the wait.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too fast.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-volume-that-was-not-orphaned\">Incident: The volume that was not orphaned\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A clean up script deleted unattached volumes and a monthly reporting job failed the next week.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters Name=status,Values=available --query &quot;Volumes[].[VolumeId,CreateTime]&quot; --output text\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters Name=status,Values=available --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Volumes[].[VolumeId,CreateTime]&quot;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vol-0a1b 2026-08-02\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">vol-0c2d 2026-09-14\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The volumes were unattached because the reporting job attached them only for a few hours at the start of each month, so a thirty day check could not tell the difference. The team restored the volume from a snapshot taken before deletion. They changed the process to tag candidates and notify owners first, to stop before deleting and to require a snapshot and a waiting period.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deleting on a single average number\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a window that misses month end\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Removing resources without telling the owner\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rightsizing memory without memory metrics\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making schedules impossible to override\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"common-sources","text":"Common sources"},{"id":"rightsizing","text":"Rightsizing"},{"id":"scheduling","text":"Scheduling"},{"id":"orphans","text":"Orphans"},{"id":"safe-removal","text":"Safe removal"},{"id":"incident-the-volume-that-was-not-orphaned","text":"Incident: The volume that was not orphaned"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS right sizing","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/aws-cost-management/aws-cost-optimization/right-sizing/"},{"t":"Azure advisor cost recommendations","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/advisor/advisor-cost-recommendations"},{"t":"OpenCost","u":"https://www.opencost.io/docs/"}],"text":"Waste is the cheapest saving because it needs no change of design. It is also easy to cause an outage while removing it, so the process matters as much as the search. What you will learn The usual sources of waste Rightsizing with real utilisation data Scheduling non production environments Orphaned volumes, snapshots, addresses and load balancers A safe removal process with an undo path Common sources Typical waste includes stopped instances that still have paid disks, detached volumes, old snapshots, unused public addresses, idle load balancers, test clusters that nobody uses and over provisioned databases. Each is small, but together they often reach ten to twenty percent of a bill. Rightsizing Compare what a resource has with what it uses over a representative period, including peaks such as month end. Look at CPU, memory, disk and network, using percentiles and not only averages. Provider tools give recommendations, and you should confirm them against application behaviour, because memory is often invisible to default metrics. Change one step at a time. Scheduling Development and test environments are needed during working hours and not at night or at weekends. Stopping them automatically on a schedule saves most of their cost. Make the schedule easy to override, so a team working late can start their environment without a ticket. Orphans Resources lose their owner when projects end. Find them with queries such as volumes with no attachment for thirty days or load balancers with no healthy targets. Check the creation time and tags, and contact the owner or the team that owned the account before deleting anything. Safe removal Use a staged process: tag as candidate for deletion with a date, notify the owner, stop or detach first, snapshot where it matters, wait a defined period and then delete. Record each action so that a mistake can be reversed. Never delete on the basis of cost alone without an owner decision. Finding waste: idle, orphaned and oversized resources Flow in order: 1, Utilisation scan to Candidates; 2, Candidates to Stop or detach; 3, Stop or detach to Delete. Observability Step Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Utilisation scan Observability What it does: Percentiles. If it fails or is weak: Short window. Candidates Step What it does: Tag and notify. If it fails or is weak: No owner. Stop or detach Step What it does: Reversible step. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Delete Service What it does: After the wait. If it fails or is weak: Too fast. Incident: The volume that was not orphaned Incident A clean up script deleted unattached volumes and a monthly reporting job failed the next week. $ aws ec2 describe-volumes --filters Name=status,Values=available --query \"Volumes[].[VolumeId,CreateTime]\" --output text vol-0a1b 2026-08-02 vol-0c2d 2026-09-14 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The volumes were unattached because the reporting job attached them only for a few hours at the start of each month, so a thirty day check could not tell the difference. The team restored the volume from a snapshot taken before deletion. They changed the process to tag candidates and notify owners first, to stop before deleting and to require a snapshot and a waiting period. Common mistakes Deleting on a single average number Using a window that misses month end Removing resources without telling the owner Rightsizing memory without memory metrics Making schedules impossible to override"},"finops/data-transfer-storage-tiers-and-network-cost":{"html":"\u003cp>Compute is visible, but data movement is billed per gigabyte and hides in plain sight. A design that looks neat on a diagram can send terabytes across a paid boundary every day.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What data transfer costs and where\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>NAT gateways, endpoints and private paths\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Storage classes and lifecycle rules\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Logs, backups and snapshots that grow quietly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Designing to keep traffic cheap\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-transfer-is-charged\">Where transfer is charged\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Traffic into a cloud is usually free. Traffic out to the internet is charged per gigabyte. Traffic between regions is charged, and traffic between availability zones is often charged in both directions. Traffic inside a zone is normally free. Learn the rules for your provider, because they differ in detail and change over time.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"nat-and-endpoints\">NAT and endpoints\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A NAT gateway charges per hour and per gigabyte processed, so a service that downloads large files from object storage through it pays twice. Gateway or private endpoints to the storage and registry services avoid that path. Check the flow logs for the biggest talkers before choosing a fix.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"storage-tiers\">Storage tiers\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Object storage has classes for frequent, infrequent and archive access, with lower storage prices but retrieval fees and minimum durations. Lifecycle rules move or expire objects by age or prefix automatically. Use intelligent tiering when access patterns are unknown, and avoid moving tiny objects, where request fees can exceed the saving.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"quiet-growth\">Quiet growth\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Logs, backups and snapshots grow for ever unless something removes them. Set retention for each log group, expire old snapshots with a policy and compress what you keep. Check storage by prefix or bucket each month and look for the one that doubled.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"design-for-cheap-traffic\">Design for cheap traffic\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Keep chatty services in the same zone when reliability allows, cache at the edge, compress payloads, and put the processing next to the data instead of copying data to the processing. For disaster recovery, replicate only what the recovery objective requires.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"app&gt;nat;nat&gt;store;store&gt;net\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Data transfer, storage tiers and network cost\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"app\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Application\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"nat\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">NAT or endpoint\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"store\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Object storage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"net\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Internet or region\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Application to NAT or endpoint; 2, NAT or endpoint to Object storage; 3, Object storage to Internet or region.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"app\">\u003cdt>Application \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sends data.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Chatty.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"nat\">\u003cdt>NAT or endpoint \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Per GB charge.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Wrong path.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"store\">\u003cdt>Object storage \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Tiers and lifecycle.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No rules.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"net\">\u003cdt>Internet or region \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Egress charge.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unplanned.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-nat-gateway-that-cost-more-than-the-servers\">Incident: The NAT gateway that cost more than the servers\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new data job doubled the network line of the bill within a week.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways --query &quot;NatGateways[].NatGatewayId&quot; --output text\naws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/NATGateway --metric-name BytesOutToDestination --dimensions Name=NatGatewayId,Value=nat-0abc123 --start-time 2026-11-01T00:00:00Z --end-time 2026-11-08T00:00:00Z --period 86400 --statistics Sum\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways --query \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;NatGateways[].NatGatewayId&quot;\u003c/span> --output text\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">nat-0abc123\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/NATGateway --metric-name BytesOutToDestination --dimensions Name=NatGatewayId,Value=nat-0abc123 --start-time 2026-11-01T00:00:00Z --end-time 2026-11-08T00:00:00Z --period 86400 --statistics Sum\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Sum 9.2e12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The job read hundreds of gigabytes a day from object storage in the same region, but through the NAT gateway, which charged for every gigabyte. Adding a gateway endpoint for the storage service sent that traffic over a private path at no processing charge. The team then added a cost check for any new job that moves more than a set volume.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ignoring cross zone traffic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reading object storage through a NAT gateway\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Archive tiers for data that is read often\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No retention on logs and snapshots\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copying all data to a second region by default\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-transfer-is-charged","text":"Where transfer is charged"},{"id":"nat-and-endpoints","text":"NAT and endpoints"},{"id":"storage-tiers","text":"Storage tiers"},{"id":"quiet-growth","text":"Quiet growth"},{"id":"design-for-cheap-traffic","text":"Design for cheap traffic"},{"id":"incident-the-nat-gateway-that-cost-more-than-the-servers","text":"Incident: The NAT gateway that cost more than the servers"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS data transfer pricing","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/pricing/on-demand/"},{"t":"Amazon S3 storage classes","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/s3/storage-classes/"},{"t":"Google Cloud billing export","u":"https://cloud.google.com/billing/docs/how-to/export-data-bigquery"}],"text":"Compute is visible, but data movement is billed per gigabyte and hides in plain sight. A design that looks neat on a diagram can send terabytes across a paid boundary every day. What you will learn What data transfer costs and where NAT gateways, endpoints and private paths Storage classes and lifecycle rules Logs, backups and snapshots that grow quietly Designing to keep traffic cheap Where transfer is charged Traffic into a cloud is usually free. Traffic out to the internet is charged per gigabyte. Traffic between regions is charged, and traffic between availability zones is often charged in both directions. Traffic inside a zone is normally free. Learn the rules for your provider, because they differ in detail and change over time. NAT and endpoints A NAT gateway charges per hour and per gigabyte processed, so a service that downloads large files from object storage through it pays twice. Gateway or private endpoints to the storage and registry services avoid that path. Check the flow logs for the biggest talkers before choosing a fix. Storage tiers Object storage has classes for frequent, infrequent and archive access, with lower storage prices but retrieval fees and minimum durations. Lifecycle rules move or expire objects by age or prefix automatically. Use intelligent tiering when access patterns are unknown, and avoid moving tiny objects, where request fees can exceed the saving. Quiet growth Logs, backups and snapshots grow for ever unless something removes them. Set retention for each log group, expire old snapshots with a policy and compress what you keep. Check storage by prefix or bucket each month and look for the one that doubled. Design for cheap traffic Keep chatty services in the same zone when reliability allows, cache at the edge, compress payloads, and put the processing next to the data instead of copying data to the processing. For disaster recovery, replicate only what the recovery objective requires. Data transfer, storage tiers and network cost Flow in order: 1, Application to NAT or endpoint; 2, NAT or endpoint to Object storage; 3, Object storage to Internet or region. Service Data store Outside party Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Application Service What it does: Sends data. If it fails or is weak: Chatty. NAT or endpoint Service What it does: Per GB charge. If it fails or is weak: Wrong path. Object storage Data store What it does: Tiers and lifecycle. If it fails or is weak: No rules. Internet or region Outside party What it does: Egress charge. If it fails or is weak: Unplanned. Incident: The NAT gateway that cost more than the servers Incident A new data job doubled the network line of the bill within a week. $ aws ec2 describe-nat-gateways --query \"NatGateways[].NatGatewayId\" --output text nat-0abc123 $ aws cloudwatch get-metric-statistics --namespace AWS/NATGateway --metric-name BytesOutToDestination --dimensions Name=NatGatewayId,Value=nat-0abc123 --start-time 2026-11-01T00:00:00Z --end-time 2026-11-08T00:00:00Z --period 86400 --statistics Sum Sum 9.2e12 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The job read hundreds of gigabytes a day from object storage in the same region, but through the NAT gateway, which charged for every gigabyte. Adding a gateway endpoint for the storage service sent that traffic over a private path at no processing charge. The team then added a cost check for any new job that moves more than a set volume. Common mistakes Ignoring cross zone traffic Reading object storage through a NAT gateway Archive tiers for data that is read often No retention on logs and snapshots Copying all data to a second region by default"},"finops/commitments-spot-and-the-pricing-portfolio":{"html":"\u003cp>Discounts of thirty to seventy percent are available for predictable use, but they are promises. This lesson treats pricing as a portfolio that you manage with numbers and review regularly.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The pricing options and what each one trades\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Coverage and utilisation: the two numbers to watch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Laddering commitments over time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Spot capacity and interruption design\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reviewing the portfolio every month\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-options\">The options\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>On demand has no commitment and the highest price. Reserved instances and savings plans give a discount for a commitment to a term of one or three years, either to specific capacity or to a spend per hour. Spot capacity is spare capacity at a large discount that the provider can reclaim with short notice. Each trades flexibility for price.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"coverage-and-utilisation\">Coverage and utilisation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Coverage is the share of eligible usage that a commitment covers. Utilisation is the share of the commitment that you actually use. High coverage with low utilisation means you pay for unused commitment. Low coverage means you leave discounts unused. Aim for utilisation near one hundred percent on the baseline and cover the steady floor, not the peaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"laddering\">Laddering\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Buying everything at once risks a cliff when the term ends and locks in today architecture. Buy in steps through the year, so that part of the portfolio expires every few months. Prefer flexible spend based plans for usage that may change instance family, and keep a margin for projects that will shrink.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"spot-design\">Spot design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Spot suits stateless, restartable and batch work. Diversify across instance types and zones, handle the interruption notice by draining and checkpointing, and keep a small on demand base for work that cannot stop. Kubernetes tools and managed groups can mix both for you. Do not run a single replica database on spot.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"monthly-review\">Monthly review\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Review coverage, utilisation, expiring terms and forecast usage once a month. Record who owns the decision and the rules, such as the maximum share committed. Share the savings and the unused commitment openly with the teams, so that they can plan to use what has been bought.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"base&gt;flex;flex&gt;spot;spot&gt;rev\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Commitments, spot and the pricing portfolio\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"base\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Steady baseline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"flex\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Variable load\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"spot\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Spot batch\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Monthly review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Steady baseline to Variable load; 2, Variable load to Spot batch; 3, Spot batch to Monthly review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"base\">\u003cdt>Steady baseline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Committed.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Shrinks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"flex\">\u003cdt>Variable load \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> On demand.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Costly.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"spot\">\u003cdt>Spot batch \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Interruptible.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Reclaimed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Monthly review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Coverage and use.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-a-three-year-commitment-for-a-service-that-shrank\">Incident: A three year commitment for a service that shrank\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A large commitment was bought and the service it covered moved to another instance family.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ce get-savings-plans-utilization --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-11-01 --query Total.Utilization.UtilizationPercentage\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ce get-savings-plans-utilization --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-11-01 --query Total.Utilization.UtilizationPercentage\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">61.4\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The commitment was sized to peak usage and was tied to one instance family. After a migration to a newer family and a project that ended, only sixty one percent was used, so the company paid for capacity it did not need for the rest of the term. The team moved to flexible spend based plans, bought in quarterly steps and added utilisation to the monthly review, with an alert below ninety percent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Committing to the peak instead of the floor\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Locking into one instance family for three years\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Buying everything on one day\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running stateful single replicas on spot\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not reviewing utilisation after a migration\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-options","text":"The options"},{"id":"coverage-and-utilisation","text":"Coverage and utilisation"},{"id":"laddering","text":"Laddering"},{"id":"spot-design","text":"Spot design"},{"id":"monthly-review","text":"Monthly review"},{"id":"incident-a-three-year-commitment-for-a-service-that-shrank","text":"Incident: A three year commitment for a service that shrank"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Savings Plans","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/savingsplans/"},{"t":"Amazon EC2 Spot Instances","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/ec2/spot/"},{"t":"Azure Cost Management","u":"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/"}],"text":"Discounts of thirty to seventy percent are available for predictable use, but they are promises. This lesson treats pricing as a portfolio that you manage with numbers and review regularly. What you will learn The pricing options and what each one trades Coverage and utilisation: the two numbers to watch Laddering commitments over time Spot capacity and interruption design Reviewing the portfolio every month The options On demand has no commitment and the highest price. Reserved instances and savings plans give a discount for a commitment to a term of one or three years, either to specific capacity or to a spend per hour. Spot capacity is spare capacity at a large discount that the provider can reclaim with short notice. Each trades flexibility for price. Coverage and utilisation Coverage is the share of eligible usage that a commitment covers. Utilisation is the share of the commitment that you actually use. High coverage with low utilisation means you pay for unused commitment. Low coverage means you leave discounts unused. Aim for utilisation near one hundred percent on the baseline and cover the steady floor, not the peaks. Laddering Buying everything at once risks a cliff when the term ends and locks in today architecture. Buy in steps through the year, so that part of the portfolio expires every few months. Prefer flexible spend based plans for usage that may change instance family, and keep a margin for projects that will shrink. Spot design Spot suits stateless, restartable and batch work. Diversify across instance types and zones, handle the interruption notice by draining and checkpointing, and keep a small on demand base for work that cannot stop. Kubernetes tools and managed groups can mix both for you. Do not run a single replica database on spot. Monthly review Review coverage, utilisation, expiring terms and forecast usage once a month. Record who owns the decision and the rules, such as the maximum share committed. Share the savings and the unused commitment openly with the teams, so that they can plan to use what has been bought. Commitments, spot and the pricing portfolio Flow in order: 1, Steady baseline to Variable load; 2, Variable load to Spot batch; 3, Spot batch to Monthly review. Data store Service Observability Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Steady baseline Data store What it does: Committed. If it fails or is weak: Shrinks. Variable load Service What it does: On demand. If it fails or is weak: Costly. Spot batch Service What it does: Interruptible. If it fails or is weak: Reclaimed. Monthly review Observability What it does: Coverage and use. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Incident: A three year commitment for a service that shrank Incident A large commitment was bought and the service it covered moved to another instance family. $ aws ce get-savings-plans-utilization --time-period Start=2026-10-01,End=2026-11-01 --query Total.Utilization.UtilizationPercentage 61.4 (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) The commitment was sized to peak usage and was tied to one instance family. After a migration to a newer family and a project that ended, only sixty one percent was used, so the company paid for capacity it did not need for the rest of the term. The team moved to flexible spend based plans, bought in quarterly steps and added utilisation to the monthly review, with an alert below ninety percent. Common mistakes Committing to the peak instead of the floor Locking into one instance family for three years Buying everything on one day Running stateful single replicas on spot Not reviewing utilisation after a migration"},"finops/budgets-anomalies-forecasts-and-cost-culture":{"html":"\u003cp>Savings do not last without routines. Costs drift back up when nobody is watching, so the final step is to build alerts, reviews and incentives that keep the numbers honest.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Budgets with owners and thresholds\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Anomaly detection and fast response\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forecasting from usage drivers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Showback and chargeback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making cost a normal engineering topic\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"budgets\">Budgets\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Set a budget per team or service that reflects the plan, with alerts at fifty, eighty and one hundred percent of forecast spend. Send alerts to the owning team channel, not to a mailbox that nobody reads. A budget that is exceeded should start a conversation about whether the plan or the spend should change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"anomalies\">Anomalies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Anomaly detection compares today with a pattern for the same service or account and alerts on an unusual jump. Combine the provider tools with simple checks of your own, such as daily cost by account against a rolling average. The target is to notice within a day, because one forgotten cluster costs the same each day that it runs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"forecasting\">Forecasting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Forecast from drivers, such as expected traffic, new customers and planned launches, and not only by extending last month. Compare the forecast with the result each month and write down why they differed. The error will shrink as the model improves, and the review teaches the team which drivers matter.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"showback-and-chargeback\">Showback and chargeback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Showback reports cost to teams without moving money. Chargeback actually bills teams internally. Start with showback, because it builds trust and finds data problems before money is at stake. Move to chargeback only when allocation is accurate and teams can control what they are charged for.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"culture\">Culture\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Make cost visible where engineers work: in pull requests that change infrastructure, in dashboards beside latency and in design reviews. Celebrate savings that came from better design. Avoid blame, because a team that fears punishment hides spend. Give teams the data, the authority and a goal they believe is fair.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"bud&gt;ano;ano&gt;fc;fc&gt;team\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Budgets, anomaly alerts, forecasts and cost culture\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bud\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Budgets\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ano\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Anomaly alerts\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"fc\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Forecast\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"team\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:75.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Team review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Budgets to Anomaly alerts; 2, Anomaly alerts to Forecast; 3, Forecast to Team review.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bud\">\u003cdt>Budgets \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owner thresholds.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unowned.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ano\">\u003cdt>Anomaly alerts \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Daily check.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Late.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"fc\">\u003cdt>Forecast \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Drivers.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"team\">\u003cdt>Team review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Weekly habit.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-experiment-that-ran-for-twenty-three-days\">Incident: The experiment that ran for twenty three days\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A training cluster started for a test was never stopped and the cost was found at month end.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"aws ce get-anomalies --date-interval StartDate=2026-10-01,EndDate=2026-10-31 --max-results 5\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>aws ce get-anomalies --date-interval StartDate=2026-10-01,EndDate=2026-10-31 --max-results 5\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">No anomaly monitors are configured for this account\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>No anomaly monitor existed, and the monthly report was read only after the month closed. The cluster ran for twenty three days before a finance review noticed. The team created anomaly monitors per account with alerts to the owner channel, a daily cost chart per team and an auto stop tag for experiments. The next similar mistake was found in one day.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Budgets sent to an unread mailbox\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Waiting for month end to look at cost\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Moving to chargeback before allocation is accurate\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Blaming teams for surprises\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forecasting only by copying last month\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"budgets","text":"Budgets"},{"id":"anomalies","text":"Anomalies"},{"id":"forecasting","text":"Forecasting"},{"id":"showback-and-chargeback","text":"Showback and chargeback"},{"id":"culture","text":"Culture"},{"id":"incident-the-experiment-that-ran-for-twenty-three-days","text":"Incident: The experiment that ran for twenty three days"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"AWS Budgets","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/aws-cost-management/aws-budgets/"},{"t":"AWS Cost Anomaly Detection","u":"https://aws.amazon.com/aws-cost-management/aws-cost-anomaly-detection/"},{"t":"FinOps Foundation framework","u":"https://www.finops.org/framework/"}],"text":"Savings do not last without routines. Costs drift back up when nobody is watching, so the final step is to build alerts, reviews and incentives that keep the numbers honest. What you will learn Budgets with owners and thresholds Anomaly detection and fast response Forecasting from usage drivers Showback and chargeback Making cost a normal engineering topic Budgets Set a budget per team or service that reflects the plan, with alerts at fifty, eighty and one hundred percent of forecast spend. Send alerts to the owning team channel, not to a mailbox that nobody reads. A budget that is exceeded should start a conversation about whether the plan or the spend should change. Anomalies Anomaly detection compares today with a pattern for the same service or account and alerts on an unusual jump. Combine the provider tools with simple checks of your own, such as daily cost by account against a rolling average. The target is to notice within a day, because one forgotten cluster costs the same each day that it runs. Forecasting Forecast from drivers, such as expected traffic, new customers and planned launches, and not only by extending last month. Compare the forecast with the result each month and write down why they differed. The error will shrink as the model improves, and the review teaches the team which drivers matter. Showback and chargeback Showback reports cost to teams without moving money. Chargeback actually bills teams internally. Start with showback, because it builds trust and finds data problems before money is at stake. Move to chargeback only when allocation is accurate and teams can control what they are charged for. Culture Make cost visible where engineers work: in pull requests that change infrastructure, in dashboards beside latency and in design reviews. Celebrate savings that came from better design. Avoid blame, because a team that fears punishment hides spend. Give teams the data, the authority and a goal they believe is fair. Budgets, anomaly alerts, forecasts and cost culture Flow in order: 1, Budgets to Anomaly alerts; 2, Anomaly alerts to Forecast; 3, Forecast to Team review. Step Observability Service Numbered arrows show the order of the flow Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. Budgets Step What it does: Owner thresholds. If it fails or is weak: Unowned. Anomaly alerts Observability What it does: Daily check. If it fails or is weak: Late. Forecast Observability What it does: Drivers. If it fails or is weak: Stale. Team review Service What it does: Weekly habit. If it fails or is weak: Skipped. Incident: The experiment that ran for twenty three days Incident A training cluster started for a test was never stopped and the cost was found at month end. $ aws ce get-anomalies --date-interval StartDate=2026-10-01,EndDate=2026-10-31 --max-results 5 No anomaly monitors are configured for this account (this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident) No anomaly monitor existed, and the monthly report was read only after the month closed. The cluster ran for twenty three days before a finance review noticed. The team created anomaly monitors per account with alerts to the owner channel, a daily cost chart per team and an auto stop tag for experiments. The next similar mistake was found in one day. Common mistakes Budgets sent to an unread mailbox Waiting for month end to look at cost Moving to chargeback before allocation is accurate Blaming teams for surprises Forecasting only by copying last month"}});